AMC.add({"comp": "amc12", "id": "2015A", "label": "2015 A", "problems": [{"q": "<p> What is the value of <span class=\"tex\">\\left(2^{0}-1+5^{2}+0\\right)^{-1} \\times 5?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">-125</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-120</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{24}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">25</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">-125</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">-120</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{5}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{24}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">25</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(1-1+25+0)^{-1} \\times 5=\\dfrac{1}{25} \\times 5= \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{5}}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-1"}, {"q": "<p> Two of the three sides of a triangle are <span class=\"tex\">20</span> and <span class=\"tex\">15</span>. Which of the following numbers is not a possible perimeter of the triangle?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">52</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">57</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">62</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">67</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">72</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">52</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">57</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">62</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">67</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">72</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>By the Triangle Inequality the third side must be greater than <span class=\"tex\">20-15=5</span> and less than <span class=\"tex\">20+15=35</span>. Therefore the perimeter must be greater than <span class=\"tex\">5+20+15=40</span> and less than <span class=\"tex\">35+20+15=70</span>. Among the choices only <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{72}</span> cannot be the perimeter.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-2"}, {"q": "<p> Mr. Patrick teaches math to <span class=\"tex\">15</span> students. He was grading tests and found that when he graded everyone's test except Payton's, the average grade for the class was <span class=\"tex\">80</span>. After he graded Payton's test, the class average became <span class=\"tex\">81</span>. What was Payton's score on the test?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">81</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">85</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">91</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">94</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">95</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">81</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">85</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">91</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">94</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">95</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The sum of the 14 test scores was <span class=\"tex\">14 \\cdot 80=1120</span>. The sum of all 15 test scores was <span class=\"tex\">15 \\cdot 81=1215</span>. Therefore Payton's score was <span class=\"tex\">1215-1120= \\boxed{95}</span>.</p>\n<p>OR</p>\n<p>To bring the average up to 81 , the total must include 1 more point for each of the 14 students, in addition to 81 points for Payton. Therefore Payton's score was <span class=\"tex\">81+14= \\boxed{95}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-3"}, {"q": "<p> The sum of two positive numbers is <span class=\"tex\">5</span> times their difference. What is the ratio of the larger number to the smaller?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{4}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{9}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{2}</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{4}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{2}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{9}{5}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{2}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> be the two positive numbers, with <span class=\"tex\">x&gt;y</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">x+y=5(x-y)</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">4 x=6 y</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{x}{y}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{3}{2}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-4"}, {"q": "<p> Amelia needs to estimate the quantity <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{a}{b}-c</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">a, b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> are large positive integers. She rounds each of the integers so that the calculation will be easier to do mentally. In which of these situations will her answer necessarily be greater than the exact value of <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{a}{b}-c?</span></p>", "c": ["She rounds all three numbers up.", "She rounds <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> up, and she rounds <span class=\"tex\">c</span> down.", "She rounds <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> up, and she rounds <span class=\"tex\">b</span> down.", "She rounds <span class=\"tex\">a</span> up, and she rounds <span class=\"tex\">b</span> and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> down.", "She rounds <span class=\"tex\">c</span> up, and she rounds <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> down."], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. She rounds all three numbers up.<br/>\nB. She rounds <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> up, and she rounds <span class=\"tex\">c</span> down.<br/>\nC. She rounds <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> up, and she rounds <span class=\"tex\">b</span> down.<br/>\nD. She rounds <span class=\"tex\">a</span> up, and she rounds <span class=\"tex\">b</span> and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> down.<br/>\nE. She rounds <span class=\"tex\">c</span> up, and she rounds <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> down.<br/>\n</p>\n<p>As long as <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> and their rounded values are positive, rounding the dividend <span class=\"tex\">x</span> up in a division problem <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{x}{y}</span> makes the answer larger, and rounding <span class=\"tex\">x</span> down makes the answer smaller. Similarly, rounding the divisor <span class=\"tex\">y</span> up makes the answer smaller, and rounding <span class=\"tex\">y</span> down makes the answer larger. In a subtraction problem <span class=\"tex\">x-y</span>, rounding <span class=\"tex\">x</span> up or rounding <span class=\"tex\">y</span> down increases the answer, and rounding <span class=\"tex\">x</span> down or rounding <span class=\"tex\">y</span> up decreases it. Only in choice (D) do all the roundings contribute to increasing the answer. In the other situations, the estimate may be larger or smaller than the exact value, depending on the the amount by which each number is rounded and their values. In particular, the rounding may make the answer smaller.</p>\n<p>For (A), <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{999999}{900}-490&gt;\\dfrac{1000000}{1000}-500</span>.</p>\n<p>For (B), <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{999999}{900}-510&gt;\\dfrac{1000000}{1000}-500</span>.</p>\n<p>For (C), <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{999999}{1001}-490&gt;\\dfrac{1000000}{1000}-500</span>.</p>\n<p>For (E), <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1000001}{1001}-490&gt;\\dfrac{1000000}{1000}-500</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-5"}, {"q": "<p> Two years ago Pete was three times as old as his cousin Claire. Two years before that, Pete was four times as old as Claire. In how many years will the ratio of their ages be <span class=\"tex\">2: 1?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">5</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">6</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">8</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">p</span> be Pete's present age, and let <span class=\"tex\">c</span> be Claire's age. Then <span class=\"tex\">p-2=3(c-2)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">p-4=4(c-4)</span>. Solving these equations gives <span class=\"tex\">p=20</span> and<br/>\n<span class=\"tex\">c=8</span>. Thus Pete is 12 years older than Claire, so the ratio of their ages will be <span class=\"tex\">2: 1</span> when Claire is 12 years old. That will occur <span class=\"tex\">12-8= \\boxed{4}</span> years from now.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-6"}, {"q": "<p> Two right circular cylinders have the same volume. The radius of the second cylinder is <span class=\"tex\">10 \\%</span> more than the radius of the first. What is the relationship between the heights of the two cylinders?</p>", "c": ["The second height is <span class=\"tex\">10 \\%</span> less than the first.", "The first height is <span class=\"tex\">10 \\%</span> more than the second.", "The second height is <span class=\"tex\">21 \\%</span> less than the first.", "The first height is <span class=\"tex\">21 \\%</span> more than the second.", "The second height is <span class=\"tex\">80 \\%</span> of the first."], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. $The second height is <span class=\"tex\">10 \\%</span> less than the first.$<br/>\nB. $The first height is <span class=\"tex\">10 \\%</span> more than the second.$<br/>\nC. $The second height is <span class=\"tex\">21 \\%</span> less than the first.$<br/>\nD. $The first height is <span class=\"tex\">21 \\%</span> more than the second.$<br/>\nE. $The second height is <span class=\"tex\">80 \\%</span> of the first.$<br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">r, h, R, H</span> be the radii and heights of the first and second cylinders, respectively. The volumes are equal, so <span class=\"tex\">\\pi r^{2} h=\\pi R^{2} H</span>. Also <span class=\"tex\">R=</span> <span class=\"tex\">r+0.1 r=1.1 r</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">\\pi r^{2} h=\\pi(1.1 r)^{2} H=\\pi\\left(1.21 r^{2}\\right) H</span>. Dividing by <span class=\"tex\">\\pi r^{2}</span> yields <span class=\"tex\">h=1.21 H=H+0.21 H</span>. Thus the first height is <span class=\"tex\">21 \\%</span> more than the second height.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-7"}, {"q": "<p> The ratio of the length to the width of a rectangle is <span class=\"tex\">4: 3</span>. If the rectangle has diagonal of length <span class=\"tex\">d</span>, then the area may be expressed as <span class=\"tex\">k d^{2}</span> for some constant <span class=\"tex\">k</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">k?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{12}{25}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{16}{25}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{4}</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{7}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{7}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{12}{25}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{16}{25}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{4}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let the sides of the rectangle have lengths <span class=\"tex\">3 a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">4 a</span>. By the Pythagorean Theorem, the diagonal has length <span class=\"tex\">5 a</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">5 a=d</span>, the side lengths are <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{5} d</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{4}{5} d</span>. Therefore the area is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{5} d \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{5} d=\\dfrac{12}{25} d^{2}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">k= \\boxed{\\dfrac{12}{25}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-8"}, {"q": "<p> A box contains <span class=\"tex\">2</span> red marbles, <span class=\"tex\">2</span> green marbles, and <span class=\"tex\">2</span> yellow marbles. Carol takes <span class=\"tex\">2</span> marbles from the box at random; then Claudia takes <span class=\"tex\">2</span> of the remaining marbles at random; and then Cheryl takes the last <span class=\"tex\">2</span> marbles. What is the probability that Cheryl gets <span class=\"tex\">2</span> marbles of the same color?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{10}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{6}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{10}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{6}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{5}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{3}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Because the marbles left for Cheryl are determined at random, the second of Cheryl's marbles is equally likely to be any of the 5 marbles other than her first marble. One of those 5 marbles matches her first marble in color. Therefore the probability is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{5}}</span>.</p>\n<p>OR</p>\n<p>Because all the choices are made at random, Cheryl is equally likely to take any of the <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\begin{array}{l}6 \\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)=15</span> possible pairs of marbles. Exactly 3 of these are pairs of same-colored marbles. Therefore the requested probability is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{15}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{5}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-9"}, {"q": "<p> Integers <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> with <span class=\"tex\">x&gt;y&gt;0</span> satisfy <span class=\"tex\">x+y+x y=80</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">x?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">15</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">18</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">26</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">8</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">10</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">15</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">18</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">26</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Adding 1 to both sides of the equation and factoring yields <span class=\"tex\">(x+1)(y+1)=81=3^{4}</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> are distinct positive integers and <span class=\"tex\">x&gt;y</span>, the only possibility is that <span class=\"tex\">x+1=3^{3}=27</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y+1=3^{1}=3</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">x= \\boxed{26}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-10"}, {"q": "<p> On a sheet of paper, Isabella draws a circle of radius <span class=\"tex\">2</span>, a circle of radius <span class=\"tex\">3</span>, and all possible lines simultaneously tangent to both circles. Isabella notices that she has drawn exactly <span class=\"tex\">k \\geq 0</span> lines. How many different values of <span class=\"tex\">k</span> are possible?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">5</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">6</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>If the smaller circle is in the interior of the larger circle, there are no common tangent lines. If the smaller circle is internally tangent to the larger circle, there is exactly one common tangent line. If the circles intersect at two points, there are exactly two common tangent lines. If the circles are externally tangent, there are exactly three tangent lines. Finally, if the circles do not intersect, there are exactly four tangent lines. Therefore, <span class=\"tex\">k</span> can be any of the numbers <span class=\"tex\">0,1,2,3</span>, or 4 , which gives <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{5}</span> possibilities.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-11"}, {"q": "<p> The parabolas <span class=\"tex\">y=a x^{2}-2</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y=4-b x^{2}</span> intersect the coordinate axes in exactly four points, and these four points are the vertices of a kite of area <span class=\"tex\">12</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">a+b?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1.5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2.5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">1</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">1.5</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">2.5</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The <span class=\"tex\">y</span>-intercepts of the two parabolas are -2 and 4 , respectively,<br/>\nand in order to intersect the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis, the first must open upward and the second downward. Because the area of the kite is 12 , the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-intercepts of both parabolas must be -2 and 2 . Thus <span class=\"tex\">4 a-2=0</span> so <span class=\"tex\">a=\\dfrac{1}{2}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">4-4 b=0</span> so <span class=\"tex\">b=1</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">a+b= \\boxed{1.5}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-12"}, {"q": "<p> A league with <span class=\"tex\">12</span> teams holds a round-robin tournament, with each team playing every other team exactly once. Games either end with one team victorious or else end in a draw. A team scores <span class=\"tex\">2</span> points for every game it wins and <span class=\"tex\">1</span> point for every game it draws. Which of the following is not a true statement about the list of <span class=\"tex\">12</span> scores?</p>", "c": ["There must be an even number of odd scores.", "There must be an even number of even scores.", "There cannot be two scores of <span class=\"tex\">0</span>.", "The sum of the scores must be at least <span class=\"tex\">100</span>.", "The highest score must be at least <span class=\"tex\">12</span>."], "a": "E", "s": "<p>A. There must be an even number of odd scores.<br/>\nB. There must be an even number of even scores.<br/>\nC. There cannot be two scores of 0 .<br/>\nD. The sum of the scores must be at least 100 .<br/>\nE. The highest score must be at least 12.<br/>\n</p>\n<p>Note that each of the 12 teams plays 11 games, so <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{12 \\cdot 11}{2}=66</span> games are played in all. If every game ends in a draw, then each team will have a score of 11, so statement <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{(E)}</span> is not true. Each of the other statements is true. Each of the games generates 2 points in the score list, regardless of its outcome, so the sum of the scores must be <span class=\"tex\">66 \\cdot 2=132</span>; thus (D) is true. Because the sum of an odd number of odd numbers plus any number of even numbers is odd, and 132 is even, there must be an even number of odd scores; thus (A) is true. Because there are 12 scores in all, there must also be an even number of even scores; thus (B) is true. Two teams cannot both have a score of 0 because the game between them must result in 1 point for each of them or 2 points for one of them; thus (C) is true.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-13"}, {"q": "<p> What is the value of <span class=\"tex\">a</span> for which <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{\\log _{2} a}+\\dfrac{1}{\\log _{3} a}+\\dfrac{1}{\\log _{4} a}=1?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">9</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">12</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">18</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">24</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">36</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">9</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">12</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">18</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">24</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">36</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>By the change of base formula, <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{\\log _{m} n}=\\log _{n} m</span>. Thus</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">1=\\dfrac{1}{\\log _{2} a}+\\dfrac{1}{\\log _{3} a}+\\dfrac{1}{\\log _{4} a}=\\log _{a} 2+\\log _{a} 3+\\log _{a} 4=\\log _{a} 24</span></p><p>It follows that <span class=\"tex\">a= \\boxed{24}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-14"}, {"q": "<p> What is the minimum number of digits to the right of the decimal point needed to express the fraction <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{123456789}{2^{26} \\cdot 5^{4}}</span> as a decimal?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">22</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">26</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">30</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">104</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">22</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">26</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">30</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">104</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The numerator and denominator of this fraction have no common factors. To express the fraction as a decimal requires rewriting it with a power of 10 as the denominator. The smallest denominator that permits this is <span class=\"tex\">10^{26}</span> :</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{123456789}{2^{26} \\cdot 5^{4}}=\\dfrac{123456789 \\cdot 5^{22}}{2^{26} \\cdot 5^{4} \\cdot 5^{22}}=\\dfrac{123456789 \\cdot 5^{22}}{10^{26}}</span></p><p>so the numeral will have <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{26}</span> places after the decimal point. In fact</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{123456789}{2^{26} \\cdot 5^{4}}=0.00294343922138214111328125</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-15"}, {"q": "<p> Tetrahedron <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> has <span class=\"tex\">A B=5, A C=3, B C=4, B D=4, A D=3</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">C D=\\dfrac{12}{5} \\sqrt{2}</span>. What is the volume of the tetrahedron?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">3 \\sqrt{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2 \\sqrt{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{24}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3 \\sqrt{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{24}{5} \\sqrt{2}</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">3 \\sqrt{2}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">2 \\sqrt{5}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{24}{5}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">3 \\sqrt{3}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{24}{5} \\sqrt{2}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Triangles <span class=\"tex\">A B C</span> and <span class=\"tex\">A B D</span> are 3-4-5 right triangles with area 6. Let <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C E}</span> be the altitude of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">C E=\\dfrac{12}{5}</span>. Likewise in <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B D</span>, <span class=\"tex\">D E=\\dfrac{12}{5}</span>. Triangle <span class=\"tex\">C D E</span> has sides <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{12}{5}, \\dfrac{12}{5}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{12}{5} \\sqrt{2}</span>, so it is an isosceles right triangle with right angle <span class=\"tex\">C E D</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{D E}</span> is the altitude of the tetrahedron to base <span class=\"tex\">A B C</span>. The tetrahedron's volume is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{3} \\cdot 6 \\cdot \\dfrac{12}{5}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{24}{5}}</span>.</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2015A/2023_11_14_2dfd49b913a04bfe086cg-110.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-16"}, {"q": "<p> Eight people are sitting around a circular table, each holding a fair coin. All eight people flip their coins and those who flip heads stand while those who flip tails remain seated. What is the probability that no two adjacent people will stand?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{47}{256}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{16}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{49}{256}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{25}{128}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{51}{256}</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{47}{256}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{16}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{49}{256}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{25}{128}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{51}{256}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>There are <span class=\"tex\">2^{8}=256</span> equally likely outcomes of the coin tosses. Classify the possible arrangements around the table according to the number of heads flipped. There is 1 possibility with no heads, and there are 8 possibilities with exactly one head. There are <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\begin{array}{l}8 \\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)=28</span> possibilities with exactly two heads, 8 of which have two adjacent heads. There are <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\begin{array}{l}8 \\\\ 3\\end{array}\\right)=56</span> possibilities with exactly three heads, of which 8 have three adjacent heads and <span class=\"tex\">8 \\cdot 4</span> have exactly two adjacent heads ( 8 possibilities to place the two adjacent heads and 4 possibilities to place the third head). Finally, there are 2 possibilities using exactly four heads where no two of them are adjacent (heads and tails must alternate). There cannot be more than four heads without two of them being adjacent. Therefore there are <span class=\"tex\">1+8+(28-8)+(56-8-32)+2=47</span> possibilities with no adjacent heads, and the probability is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\dfrac{47}{256}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-17"}, {"q": "<p> The zeros of the function <span class=\"tex\">f(x)=x^{2}-a x+2 a</span> are integers. What is the sum of the possible values of <span class=\"tex\">a?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">16</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">17</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">18</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">7</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">8</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">16</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">17</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">18</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The zeros of <span class=\"tex\">f</span> are integers and their sum is <span class=\"tex\">a</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">a</span> is an integer. If <span class=\"tex\">r</span> is an integer zero, then <span class=\"tex\">r^{2}-a r+2 a=0</span> or</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a=\\dfrac{r^{2}}{r-2}=r+2+\\dfrac{4}{r-2} \\text {. }</span></p><p>So <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{4}{r-2}=a-r-2</span> must be an integer, and the possible values of <span class=\"tex\">r</span> are 6,4 , <span class=\"tex\">3,1,0</span>, and -2 . The possible values of <span class=\"tex\">a</span> are <span class=\"tex\">9,8,0</span>, and -1 , all of which yield integer zeros of <span class=\"tex\">f</span>, and their sum is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{16}</span> .</p>\n<p>OR</p>\n<p>As above, <span class=\"tex\">a</span> must be an integer. The function <span class=\"tex\">f</span> has zeros at</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">x=\\dfrac{a \\pm \\sqrt{a^{2}-8 a}}{2}</span></p><p>These values are integers only if <span class=\"tex\">a^{2}-8 a=w^{2}</span> for some integer <span class=\"tex\">w</span>. Solving for <span class=\"tex\">a</span> in terms of <span class=\"tex\">w</span> gives <span class=\"tex\">a=4 \\pm \\sqrt{16+w^{2}}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">16+w^{2}</span> must be a perfect square. The only integer solutions for <span class=\"tex\">w</span> are 0 and \\textbackslash pm 3 , from which it follows that the values of <span class=\"tex\">a</span> are <span class=\"tex\">0,8,9</span>, and -1 , all of which yield integer values of <span class=\"tex\">x</span>. The requested sum is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{16}</span> .</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-18"}, {"q": "<p> For some positive integers <span class=\"tex\">p</span>, there is a quadrilateral <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> with positive integer side lengths, perimeter <span class=\"tex\">p</span>, right angles at <span class=\"tex\">B</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C, A B=2</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">C D=A D</span>. How many different values of <span class=\"tex\">p&lt;2015</span> are possible?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">30</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">31</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">61</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">62</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">63</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">30</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">31</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">61</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">62</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">63</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>In every such quadrilateral, <span class=\"tex\">C D \\geq A B</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">E</span> be the foot of the perpendicular from <span class=\"tex\">A</span> to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span>; then <span class=\"tex\">C E=2</span> and <span class=\"tex\">A E=B C</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">x=A E</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y=D E</span>; then <span class=\"tex\">A D=2+y</span>. By the Pythagorean Theorem, <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+y^{2}=(2+y)^{2}</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}=4+4 y</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">x</span> is even, say <span class=\"tex\">x=2 z</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">z^{2}=1+y</span>. The perimeter of the quadrilateral is <span class=\"tex\">x+2 y+6=2 z^{2}+2 z+4</span>. Increasing positive integer values of <span class=\"tex\">z</span> give the required quadrilaterals, with increasing perimeter. For $z=31 the perimeter is 1988 , and for <span class=\"tex\">z=32</span> the perimeter is 2116 . Therefore there are <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{31}</span> such quadrilaterals.</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2015A/2023_11_14_2dfd49b913a04bfe086cg-111.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-19"}, {"q": "<p> Isosceles triangles <span class=\"tex\">T</span> and <span class=\"tex\">T^{\\prime}</span> are not congruent but have the same area and the same perimeter. The sides of <span class=\"tex\">T</span> have lengths of <span class=\"tex\">5,5,</span> and <span class=\"tex\">8,</span> while those of <span class=\"tex\">T^{\\prime}</span> have lengths <span class=\"tex\">a, a</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">b</span>. Which of the following numbers is closest to <span class=\"tex\">b?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">5</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">6</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">8</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">g</span> and <span class=\"tex\">h</span> be the lengths of the altitudes of <span class=\"tex\">T</span> and <span class=\"tex\">T^{\\prime}</span> from the sides with lengths 8 and <span class=\"tex\">b</span>, respectively. The Pythagorean Theorem implies that <span class=\"tex\">g=\\sqrt{5^{2}-4^{2}}=3</span>, and so the area of <span class=\"tex\">T</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 3=12</span>, and the perimeter is <span class=\"tex\">5+5+8=18</span>. The Pythagorean Theorem implies that <span class=\"tex\">h=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\sqrt{4 a^{2}-b^{2}}</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">18=2 a+b</span> and</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">12=\\dfrac{1}{2} b \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\sqrt{4 a^{2}-b^{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{4} b \\sqrt{4 a^{2}-b^{2}}</span></p><p>Solving for <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and substituting in the square of the second equation yields</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n12^{2} =\\dfrac{b^{2}}{16}\\left(4 a^{2}-b^{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{b^{2}}{16}\\left((18-b)^{2}-b^{2}\\right) \\\\\n=\\dfrac{b^{2}}{16} \\cdot 18 \\cdot(18-2 b)=\\dfrac{9}{4} b^{2}(9-b)\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Thus <span class=\"tex\">64-b^{2}(9-b)=b^{3}-9 b^{2}+64=(b-8)\\left(b^{2}-b-8\\right)=0</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">T</span> and <span class=\"tex\">T^{\\prime}</span> are not congruent, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">b \\neq 8</span>. Hence <span class=\"tex\">b^{2}-b-8=0</span> and the positive solution of this equation is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{33}+1)</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">25&lt;33&lt;36</span>, the solution is between <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}(5+1)=3</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}(6+1)=3.5</span>, so the closest integer is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{3}</span> .</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-20"}, {"q": "<p> A circle of radius <span class=\"tex\">r</span> passes through both foci of, and exactly four points on, the ellipse with equation <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+16 y^{2}=16</span>. The set of all possible values of <span class=\"tex\">r</span> is an interval <span class=\"tex\">[a, b)</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">a+b?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">5 \\sqrt{2}+4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{17}+7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6 \\sqrt{2}+3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{15}+8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">12</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">5 \\sqrt{2}+4</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{17}+7</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">6 \\sqrt{2}+3</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{15}+8</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">12</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The ellipse with equation <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+16 y^{2}=16</span> is centered at the origin, with a major axis of length 8 and a minor axis of length 2 . If the foci have coordinates <span class=\"tex\">( \\pm c, 0)</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">c^{2}+1^{2}=4^{2}</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">c= \\pm \\sqrt{15}</span>. Any circle passing through both foci must have its center on the <span class=\"tex\">y</span>-axis; thus <span class=\"tex\">r</span> is at least as large as the distance from the foci to the <span class=\"tex\">y</span>-axis. That is, <span class=\"tex\">r \\geq \\sqrt{15}</span>. For any <span class=\"tex\">k \\geq 0</span>, the circle of radius <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{k^{2}+15}</span> and center <span class=\"tex\">(0, k)</span> passes through both foci (in the interior of the ellipse) and the points <span class=\"tex\">\\left(0, k \\pm \\sqrt{k^{2}+15}\\right)</span>. The point <span class=\"tex\">\\left(0, k+\\sqrt{k^{2}+15}\\right)</span> is in the exterior of the ellipse since <span class=\"tex\">k+\\sqrt{k^{2}+15}&gt;\\sqrt{15}&gt;</span> 1. The point <span class=\"tex\">\\left(0, k-\\sqrt{k^{2}+15}\\right)</span> is in the exterior of the ellipse if and only if <span class=\"tex\">k-\\sqrt{k^{2}+15}&lt;-1</span>, that is, if and only if <span class=\"tex\">k&lt;7</span>. Thus, for <span class=\"tex\">k \\geq 0</span>, the circle with center <span class=\"tex\">(0, k)</span> intersects the ellipse in four points if and only if <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq k&lt;7</span>. As <span class=\"tex\">k</span> increases, the radius <span class=\"tex\">r=\\sqrt{k^{2}+15}</span> increases as well, so the set of possible radii is the interval <span class=\"tex\">\\left[\\sqrt{15}, \\sqrt{7^{2}+15}\\right)=[\\sqrt{15}, 8)</span>. The requested answer is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\sqrt{15}+8}</span>.</p>\n<center>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2015A/2023_11_14_2dfd49b913a04bfe086cg-112.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-21"}, {"q": "<p> For each positive integer <span class=\"tex\">n</span>, let <span class=\"tex\">S(n)</span> be the number of sequences of length <span class=\"tex\">n</span> consisting solely of the letters <span class=\"tex\">A</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B</span>, with no more than three <span class=\"tex\">A</span>s in a row and no more than three <span class=\"tex\">B</span>s in a row. What is the remainder when <span class=\"tex\">S(2015)</span> is divided by <span class=\"tex\">12?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">0</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Note that <span class=\"tex\">S(1)=2, S(2)=4</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">S(3)=8</span>. Call a sequence with <span class=\"tex\">A</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B</span> entries valid if it does not contain 4 or more consecutive symbols that are the same. For <span class=\"tex\">n \\geq 4</span>, every valid sequence of length <span class=\"tex\">n-1</span> can be extended to a valid sequence of length <span class=\"tex\">n</span> by appending a symbol different from its last symbol. Similarly, valid sequences of length <span class=\"tex\">n-2</span> or <span class=\"tex\">n-3</span> can be extended<br/>\nto valid sequences of length <span class=\"tex\">n</span> by appending either two or three equal symbols different from its last symbol. Note that all of these sequences are pairwise distinct. Conversely, every valid sequence of length <span class=\"tex\">n</span> ends with either one, two, or three equal consecutive symbols. Removal of these equal symbols at the end produces every valid sequence of length <span class=\"tex\">n-1, n-2</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">n-3</span>, respectively. Thus <span class=\"tex\">S(n)=S(n-1)+S(n-2)+S(n-3)</span>. This recursive formula implies that the remainders modulo 3 of the sequence <span class=\"tex\">S(n)</span> for <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq n \\leq 16</span> are</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">2,1,2,2,2,0,1,0,1,2,0,0,2,2,1,2</span></p><p>Thus the sequence is periodic with period-length 13 . Because <span class=\"tex\">2015=13 \\cdot 155</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">S(2015) \\equiv S(13) \\equiv 2(\\bmod 3)</span>. Similarly, the remainders modulo 4 of the sequence <span class=\"tex\">S(n)</span> for <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq n \\leq 7</span> are <span class=\"tex\">2,0,0,2,2,0,0</span>. Thus the sequence is periodic with period-length 4 . Because <span class=\"tex\">2015=4 \\cdot 503+3</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">S(2015) \\equiv S(3) \\equiv 0(\\bmod 4)</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">S(2015)=4 k</span> for some integer <span class=\"tex\">k</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">4 k \\equiv 2(\\bmod 3)</span>. Hence <span class=\"tex\">k \\equiv 2(\\bmod 3)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">S(2015)=4 k \\equiv 8(\\bmod 12)</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-22"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">S</span> be a square of side length <span class=\"tex\">1</span>. Two points are chosen independently at random on the sides of <span class=\"tex\">S</span>. The probability that the straight-line distance between the points is at least <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{a-b \\pi}{c}</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">a, b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> are positive integers and <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(a, b, c)=1</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">a+b+c?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">59</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">60</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">61</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">62</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">63</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">59</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">60</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">61</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">62</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">63</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let the square have vertices <span class=\"tex\">(0,0),(1,0),(1,1)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">(0,1)</span>, and consider three cases.</p>\n<p>Case 1: The chosen points are on opposite sides of the square. In this case the distance between the points is at least <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span> with probability 1 .</p>\n<p>Case 2: The chosen points are on the same side of the square. It may be assumed that the points are <span class=\"tex\">(a, 0)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(b, 0)</span>. The pairs of points in the <span class=\"tex\">a b</span>-plane that meet the requirement are those within the square <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq a \\leq 1,0 \\leq b \\leq 1</span> that satisfy either <span class=\"tex\">b \\geq a+\\dfrac{1}{2}</span> or <span class=\"tex\">b \\leq a-\\dfrac{1}{2}</span>. These inequalities describe the union of two isosceles right triangles with leg length <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span>, together with their interiors. The area of the region is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{4}</span>, and the area of the square is 1 , so the probability that the pair of points meets the requirement in this case is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{4}</span>.</p>\n<p>Case 3: The chosen points are on adjacent sides of the square. It may be assumed that the points are <span class=\"tex\">(a, 0)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(0, b)</span>. The pairs of points in the <span class=\"tex\">a b</span>-plane that meet the requirement are those within the square <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq a \\leq 1,0 \\leq b \\leq 1</span> that satisfy <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}} \\geq \\dfrac{1}{2}</span>. These inequalities describe the region inside the square and outside a quarter-circle of radius <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span>. The area of this region is <span class=\"tex\">1-\\dfrac{1}{4} \\pi\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}=1-\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}</span>, which is also the probability that the pair of points meets the requirement in this case.</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2015A/2023_11_14_2dfd49b913a04bfe086cg-113.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>\n<p>Cases 1 and 2 each occur with probability <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{4}</span>, and Case 3 occurs with probability <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span>. The requested probability is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot 1+\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{4}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}\\right)=\\dfrac{26-\\pi}{32}</span></p><p>and <span class=\"tex\">a+b+c= \\boxed{59}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-23"}, {"q": "<p> Rational numbers <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> are chosen at random among all rational numbers in the interval <span class=\"tex\">[0,2)</span> that can be written as fractions <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{n}{d}</span> where <span class=\"tex\">n</span> and <span class=\"tex\">d</span> are integers with <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq d \\leq 5</span>. What is the probability that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(\\cos (a \\pi)+i \\sin (b \\pi))^{4}</span></p><p>is a real number?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{50}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{4}{25}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{41}{200}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{6}{25}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{13}{50}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{50}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{4}{25}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{41}{200}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{6}{25}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{13}{50}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>There are 20 possible values for each of <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span>, namely those in the set</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">S=\\left\\{0,1, \\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{3}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{3}, \\dfrac{2}{3}, \\dfrac{4}{3}, \\dfrac{5}{3}, \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\dfrac{5}{4}, \\dfrac{7}{4}, \\dfrac{1}{5}, \\dfrac{2}{5}, \\dfrac{3}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\dfrac{6}{5}, \\dfrac{7}{5}, \\dfrac{8}{5}, \\dfrac{9}{5}\\right\\}</span></p><p>If <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> are real numbers, then <span class=\"tex\">(x+i y)^{2}=x^{2}-y^{2}+i(2 x y)</span> is real if and only if <span class=\"tex\">x y=0</span>, that is, <span class=\"tex\">x=0</span> or <span class=\"tex\">y=0</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">(x+i y)^{4}</span> is real if and only if <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}-y^{2}=0</span> or <span class=\"tex\">x y=0</span>, that is, <span class=\"tex\">x=0, y=0</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">x= \\pm y</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">\\left((\\cos (a \\pi)+i \\sin (b \\pi))^{4}\\right.</span> is a real number if and only if <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (a \\pi)=0, \\sin (b \\pi)=0</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (a \\pi)= \\pm \\sin (b \\pi)</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (a \\pi)=0</span> and <span class=\"tex\">a \\in S</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">a=\\dfrac{1}{2}</span> or <span class=\"tex\">a=\\dfrac{3}{2}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> has no restrictions, so there are 40 pairs <span class=\"tex\">(a, b)</span> that satisfy the condition. If <span class=\"tex\">\\sin (b \\pi)=0</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b \\in S</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">b=0</span> or <span class=\"tex\">b=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">a</span> has no restrictions, so there are 40 pairs <span class=\"tex\">(a, b)</span> that satisfy the condition, but there are 4 pairs that have been counted already, namely <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}, 0\\right),\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}, 1\\right),\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}, 0\\right)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}, 1\\right)</span>. Thus the total so far is <span class=\"tex\">40+40-4=76</span>.</p>\n<p>Note that <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (a \\pi)=\\sin (b \\pi)</span> implies that <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (a \\pi)=\\cos \\left(\\pi\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}-b\\right)\\right)</span> and thus <span class=\"tex\">a \\equiv \\dfrac{1}{2}-b(\\bmod 2)</span> or <span class=\"tex\">a \\equiv-\\dfrac{1}{2}+b(\\bmod 2)</span>. If the denominator of <span class=\"tex\">b \\in S</span> is 3 or 5 , then the denominator of <span class=\"tex\">a</span> in simplified form would be 6 or 10 , and so <span class=\"tex\">a \\notin S</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">b=\\dfrac{1}{2}</span> or <span class=\"tex\">b=\\dfrac{3}{2}</span>, then there is a unique solution to either of the two congruences, namely <span class=\"tex\">a=0</span> and <span class=\"tex\">a=1</span>, respectively. For every <span class=\"tex\">b \\in\\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{4}, \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\dfrac{5}{4}, \\dfrac{7}{4}\\right\\}</span>, there is exactly one solution <span class=\"tex\">a \\in S</span> to each of the previous congruences. None of the solutions are equal to each other because if <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}-b \\equiv-\\dfrac{1}{2}+b(\\bmod 2)</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">2 b \\equiv 1</span> <span class=\"tex\">(\\bmod 2)</span>; that is, <span class=\"tex\">b=\\dfrac{1}{2}</span> or <span class=\"tex\">b=\\dfrac{3}{2}</span>. Similarly, <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (a \\pi)=-\\sin (b \\pi)=\\sin (-b \\pi)</span> implies that <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (a \\pi)=\\cos \\left(\\pi\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}+b\\right)\\right)</span> and thus <span class=\"tex\">a \\equiv \\dfrac{1}{2}+b(\\bmod 2)</span> or <span class=\"tex\">a \\equiv-\\dfrac{1}{2}-b</span> <span class=\"tex\">(\\bmod 2)</span>. If the denominator of <span class=\"tex\">b \\in S</span> is 3 or 5 , then the denominator of <span class=\"tex\">a</span> would be 6 or 10 , and so <span class=\"tex\">a \\notin S</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">b=\\dfrac{1}{2}</span> or <span class=\"tex\">b=\\dfrac{3}{2}</span>, then there is a unique solution to either of the two congruences, namely <span class=\"tex\">a=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">a=0</span>, respectively. For every <span class=\"tex\">b \\in\\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{4}, \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\dfrac{5}{4}, \\dfrac{7}{4}\\right\\}</span>, there is exactly one solution <span class=\"tex\">a \\in S</span> to each of the previous congruences, and, as before, none of these solutions are equal to each other. Thus there are a total of <span class=\"tex\">2+8+2+8=20</span> pairs <span class=\"tex\">(a, b) \\in S^{2}</span> such that <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (a \\pi)= \\pm \\sin (b \\pi)</span>. The requested probability is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{76+20}{400}=\\dfrac{96}{400}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{6}{25}}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: By de Moivre's Theorem the fourth power of the complex number <span class=\"tex\">x+i y</span> is real if and only if it lies on one of the four lines <span class=\"tex\">x=0, y=0, x=y</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">x=-y</span>. Then the counting of <span class=\"tex\">(a, b)</span> pairs proceeds as above.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-24"}, {"q": "<p> A collection of circles in the upper half-plane, all tangent to the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis, is constructed in layers as follows. Layer <span class=\"tex\">L_{0}</span> consists of two circles of radii <span class=\"tex\">70^{2}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">73^{2}</span> that are externally tangent. For <span class=\"tex\">k \\geq 1</span>, the circles in <span class=\"tex\">\\bigcup_{j=0}^{k-1} L_{j}</span> are ordered according to their points of tangency with the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis. For every pair of consecutive circles in this order, a new circle is constructed externally tangent to each of the two circles in the pair. Layer <span class=\"tex\">L_{k}</span> consists of the <span class=\"tex\">2^{k-1}</span> circles constructed in this way. Let <span class=\"tex\">S=\\bigcup_{j=0}^{6} L_{j}</span>, and for every circle <span class=\"tex\">C</span> denote by <span class=\"tex\">r(C)</span> its radius.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2015A/2023_11_14_f0ca9305ab054e45278bg-066.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>What is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sum_{C \\in S} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r(C)}} ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{286}{35}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{583}{70}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{715}{73}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{143}{14}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1573}{146}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{286}{35}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{583}{70}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{715}{73}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{143}{14}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1573}{146}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Suppose that circles <span class=\"tex\">C_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C_{2}</span> in the upper half-plane have<br/>\ncenters <span class=\"tex\">O_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">O_{2}</span> and radii <span class=\"tex\">r_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">r_{2}</span>, respectively. Assume that <span class=\"tex\">C_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C_{2}</span> are externally tangent and tangent to the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis at <span class=\"tex\">X_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">X_{2}</span>, respectively. Let <span class=\"tex\">C</span> with center <span class=\"tex\">O</span> and radius <span class=\"tex\">r</span> be the circle externally tangent to <span class=\"tex\">C_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C_{2}</span> and tangent to the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis. Let <span class=\"tex\">X</span> be the point of tangency of <span class=\"tex\">C</span> with the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis, and let <span class=\"tex\">T_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">T_{2}</span> be the points of tangency of <span class=\"tex\">C</span> with <span class=\"tex\">C_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C_{2}</span>, respectively. Let <span class=\"tex\">M_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">M_{2}</span> be the points on the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis such that <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{M_{1} T_{1}} \\perp \\overline{O_{1} T_{1}}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{M_{2} T_{2}} \\perp \\overline{O_{2} T_{2}}</span>.</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2015A/2023_11_14_2dfd49b913a04bfe086cg-115.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>\n<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{M_{1} X_{1}}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{M_{1} T_{1}}</span> are both tangent to <span class=\"tex\">C_{1}</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">X_{1} M_{1}=</span> <span class=\"tex\">M_{1} T_{1}</span>. Similarly, <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{M_{1} T_{1}}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{M_{1} X}</span> are both tangent to <span class=\"tex\">C</span>, and thus <span class=\"tex\">M_{1} T_{1}=</span> <span class=\"tex\">M_{1} X</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">\\angle O T_{1} M_{1}, \\angle M_{1} X_{1} O_{1}, \\angle M_{1} T_{1} O</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\angle O X M_{1}</span> are all right angles and <span class=\"tex\">\\angle T_{1} M_{1} X=\\pi-\\angle X_{1} M_{1} T_{1}</span>, it follows that quadrilaterals <span class=\"tex\">O_{1} X_{1} M_{1} T_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">M_{1} X O T_{1}</span> are similar. Thus</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{r_{1}}{X_{1} M_{1}}=\\dfrac{O_{1} X_{1}}{X_{1} M_{1}}=\\dfrac{M_{1} X}{X O}=\\dfrac{X_{1} M_{1}}{r}</span></p><p>Therefore <span class=\"tex\">X_{1} M_{1}=\\sqrt{r r_{1}}</span>, and similarly <span class=\"tex\">M_{2} X_{2}=\\sqrt{r r_{2}}</span>. By the distance formula,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(r_{1}+r_{2}\\right)^{2}=\\left(O_{1} O_{2}\\right)^{2}=\\left(X_{1} X_{2}\\right)^{2}+\\left(r_{1}-r_{2}\\right)^{2}</span></p><p>Thus</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\sqrt{r_{1} r_{2}} =X_{1} X_{2}=X_{1} M_{1}+M_{1} X+X M_{2}+M_{2} X_{2} \\\\\n=2\\left(X_{1} M_{1}+M_{2} X_{2}\\right)=2 \\sqrt{r}\\left(\\sqrt{r_{1}}+\\sqrt{r_{2}}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>that is,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r_{1}}}+\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r_{2}}}</span></p><p>It follows that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sum_{C \\in L_{k}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r(C)}}=\\sum_{C \\in L_{k}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r\\left(C_{1}\\right)}}+\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r\\left(C_{2}\\right)}}\\right)</span></p><p>where <span class=\"tex\">C_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C_{2}</span> are the consecutive circles in <span class=\"tex\">\\bigcup_{j=0}^{k-1} L_{j}</span> that are tangent to <span class=\"tex\">C</span>. Note that every circle in <span class=\"tex\">\\bigcup_{j=0}^{k-1} L_{j}</span> appears twice in the sum on the right-hand side, except for the two circles in <span class=\"tex\">L_{0}</span>, which appear only once. Thus</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sum_{C \\in L_{k}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r(C)}}=2 \\sum_{j=1}^{k-1} \\sum_{C \\in L_{j}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r(C)}}+\\sum_{C \\in L_{0}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r(C)}}</span></p><p>In particular, if <span class=\"tex\">k=1</span>, then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sum_{C \\in L_{1}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r(C)}}=\\sum_{C \\in L_{0}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r(C)}}=\\dfrac{1}{70}+\\dfrac{1}{73}</span></p><p>For simplicity let <span class=\"tex\">x=\\dfrac{1}{70}+\\dfrac{1}{73}</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">k \\geq 2</span>, and suppose by induction that for <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq j \\leq k-1</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sum_{C \\in L_{j}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r(C)}}=3^{j-1} x</span></p><p>It follows that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sum_{C \\in L_{k}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r(C)}}=2\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^{k-1} 3^{j-1} x\\right)+x=2 x\\left(\\dfrac{3^{k-1}-1}{2}\\right)+x=x 3^{k-1}</span></p><p>Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{C \\in S} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r(C)}} =\\sum_{k=0}^{6} \\sum_{C \\in L_{k}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r(C)}}=x+\\sum_{k=1}^{6} x 3^{k-1}=x\\left(1+\\dfrac{3^{6}-1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n=x\\left(\\dfrac{3^{6}+1}{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{143}{70 \\cdot 73}\\left(\\dfrac{730}{2}\\right)= \\boxed{\\dfrac{143}{14}} .\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Note: Equation (1) is a special case of the Kissing Circles Theorem.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2015/part-a/problem-25"}]});
